  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Frankstown named after Frank Stevens, trading post operator, junction of Juniata and Kittanning Trail became gathering center in 1730s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://frankstowntownship.com/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "After Revolutionary War, Frankstown grew into major regional trade center; in 1820 it had 20 homes, tannery, sawmill, gristmill, distilleries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://frankstowntownship.com/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Frankstown Historical Marker on US 22 notes that Conrad Weiser in 1748 remarked, “Came to Frankstown; but saw no houses or cabins.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://explorepahistory.com/hmarker.php%3FmarkerId=1-A-20E.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Frankstown Branch Juniata River runs just south of US‑22, giving the community its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankstown%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the 1700s, the fur trade turned Indian communities along the frontier into boomtowns like Assunepachla, which sprang up near Frankstown and vanished within a generation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
